# Sealed Proofs: An Interactive Journey

Begin with the central tension: local random checks do amplify soundness, but their efficiency depends on how densely falsehood appears. Use the laboratory below to see a formula with one rejecting valuation evade repeated tests.

{{interactive_demo:0}}

## The exact finite law

The exhaustive profiler establishes the accepting fraction directly, while the formal counting algorithm explains how the complete truth table is traversed.

{{demo:1}}

{{algorithm:0}}

Compare several variable counts on one set of axes. Curves for larger $m$ remain near probability one because the single rejecting row occupies only a $2^{-m}$ fraction of the challenge space.

{{visualization:0}}

## Planning an error budget

Choose a desired error and calculate the necessary number of rounds. The repetition planner applies

$$
k=\left\lceil\frac{\log\varepsilon}{\log(1-2^{-m})}\right\rceil.
$$

{{interactive_demo:2}}

{{algorithm:1}}

The corresponding bar chart makes the exponential dependence on $m$ visible.

{{visualization:1}}

{{demo:0}}

## A separate miracle: perfect local hiding

Soundness does not imply privacy. Privacy here comes from a different symmetry: translation by a secret permutes a finite cyclic group. Explore that permutation below.

{{interactive_demo:1}}

{{visualization:2}}

The audit enumerates every secret and every mask rather than relying on sampling.

{{algorithm:2}}

{{demo:2}}

## Synthesis

The two guarantees are exact but independent. Random challenges bound the chance that falsehood escapes; fresh additive masks erase the distributional signature of a local value. Succinct private certification needs a robust encoding that spreads errors across many local tests and a simulator for the complete correlated view opened by each test.

